## Proof

**Claim:** Yes. For every entire function $f$, the function $g = e^f$ is entire and satisfies $\Re(f) = \ln|g|$.

**Construction and verification.** Let $f$ be entire and set $g(z) = e^{f(z)}$. Since $f$ is entire and the exponential is entire, $g$ is entire.

Write $f(z) = u(z) + i\,v(z)$ with $u = \Re(f)$ and $v = \Im(f)$. Then
$$g(z) = e^{f(z)} = e^{u(z) + i\,v(z)} = e^{u(z)} \cdot e^{i\,v(z)}.$$
Since $u(z) \in \mathbb{R}$, we have $|e^{u(z)}| = e^{u(z)}$, and since $v(z) \in \mathbb{R}$, we have $|e^{i\,v(z)}| = 1$. Therefore
$$|g(z)| = e^{u(z)} = e^{\Re(f(z))}.$$
Taking the natural logarithm (which is valid since $e^{u(z)} > 0$ for all $z$):
$$\ln|g(z)| = \Re(f(z)) \quad \text{for all } z \in \mathbb{C}.$$

**Remark on necessity of the form.** Conversely, suppose some entire $g$ satisfies $\Re(f) = \ln|g|$. Since $\Re(f)$ is finite and continuous everywhere, $\ln|g|$ must be finite everywhere, so $g$ has no zeros. On the simply connected domain $\mathbb{C}$, every zero-free entire function admits an entire logarithm: $g = e^{h}$ for some entire $h$. Then $\ln|g| = \Re(h)$, so $\Re(h) = \Re(f)$, meaning $h - f$ is an entire function with identically zero real part. By the open mapping theorem, a non-constant entire function has open image, but a purely imaginary-valued function has image contained in $i\mathbb{R}$ (which has empty interior); hence $h - f \equiv ic$ for some real constant $c$, giving $g = e^{ic}\,e^{f}$. Thus $g = e^{f}$ is essentially the unique solution (up to a unimodular constant factor).

### PROOF COMPLETE
